Step 3: URL Analysis
The requested URL:
https://letsdefend.io/blog/?s=skills
contains the parameter:
s=skills
The SIEM rule incorrectly matched the substring ls from the word skills and interpreted it as a possible Linux ls command execution attempt.
However:
	• No command injection indicators were identified.
	• No suspicious payloads were present in the URL.
	• No malicious outbound connections were detected.
	• Endpoint investigation showed normal browsing activity.

Step 4: Conclusion
Verdict
	• False Positive
Reason
The alert was triggered due to a keyword-based detection rule that matched the substring ls inside the word skills within the URL query parameter.
The investigation confirmed:
	• The traffic was legitimate.
	• The destination was a trusted service protected by Cloudflare.
	• No malicious behavior or command execution activity was observed on the endpoint or network logs.

Mitigation:
	• Improve SIEM rule logic to detect standalone command patterns instead of partial string matches.
Tune detection rules to avoid matching common words containing command substrings, and also test after creating the rules.
